# =============================================================================
# systemReport — settings
#
# Written and maintained by @mefamex.
# =============================================================================
#
# Read by systemReport.sh. This file IS tracked in the repository on purpose:
# the defaults live here, so a fresh clone is immediately runnable and a
# reviewer can see the shipped settings.
#
# Precedence, strongest first:
#   1. command-line arguments   (./systemReport.sh --keep 20 --nosudo)
#   2. environment variables    (SR_KEEP=20 ./systemReport.sh)
#   3. this file
#
# A `#` at the start of a line, or after a value, is a comment. Every setting
# you leave out or leave empty falls back to the built-in default shown here.
# Two or three rule lines separate the blocks, so a setting is easy to find by
# scrolling rather than by searching.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPORT_ROOT — where report folders are created
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The PARENT directory that holds the report folders, not a full report path.
# Each run opens a new <REPORT_ROOT>/arch_report_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS/ folder.
#
# Leave it empty and the script picks a location itself, first hit wins:
#   1. xdg-user-dir DOCUMENTS    honours your localised Documents folder
#   2. XDG_DOCUMENTS_DIR        read straight from ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs
#   3. ~/Documents, ~/Document  plain English names
#   4. ~/systemReport           backup inside your home directory
#   5. ~/.local/share/systemReport   last resort, exists on every account
#
# No localised name such as "Belgeler" or "Dokumente" is hardcoded anywhere in
# the script, so this also works on another machine without editing it. The
# resolved path is printed at startup, so you can always see where reports
# went.
#
# An absolute path is strongly recommended. A relative path is resolved against
# your home directory, so it means the same thing wherever you run the script.
#
# Write $HOME, not ~. A tilde is expanded by the shell when you type it as an
# argument, but this file is sourced, and a quoted "~" stays a literal tilde
# character:
#   REPORT_ROOT="~/Documents/systemReport"  ->  $HOME/~/Documents/systemReport
#   REPORT_ROOT="$HOME/Documents/systemReport"  ->  correct
#REPORT_ROOT="$HOME/systemReport"


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KEEP_REPORTS — how many reports to keep
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Once more than this many report folders exist, the oldest ones are deleted
# without asking (and printing exactly what it deleted). Set 1 to keep only the
# report you just made, or 0 to keep none at all.
#
# 10 is the default: roughly a week of reports if you collect once a day, which
# is usually enough to spot a regression without letting the folder grow
# without bound.
KEEP_REPORTS=10


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USE_SUDO_CHECKS — checks that need root
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 = run dmidecode, fdisk, smartctl, dmesg and efibootmgr.
#     Your password is asked for ONCE (sudo -v); every later sudo call in the
#     run reuses that timestamp and never prompts again.
# 0 = skip those five checks and write a "skipped (needs root)" note in their
#     place, so the report shows what is missing instead of silently omitting
#     it.
#
# If you mistype the password the script does NOT abort. It carries on
# unprivileged: a partial report is more useful than no report.
USE_SUDO_CHECKS=1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLEANUP_ENABLED — deleting old reports
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 = delete report folders beyond KEEP_REPORTS.
# 0 = touch nothing. (--noclean sets the same thing for one run.)
#
# Only folders this tool created are ever considered, and the folder belonging
# to the current run is never a candidate.
CLEANUP_ENABLED=1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOG_FILE — terminal log
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every line printed to the terminal is also written here, without ANSI colour
# codes, so the log stays greppable and safe to paste into a bug report.
#
# The path below is left EMPTY on purpose, and that is the setting you want:
# the log is written inside each report, next to the files it describes.
#
#   ~/Belgeler/systemReport/arch_report_20261001_195437/
#   ├── 00_privacy.txt
#   ├── ...
#   ├── arch_full_report.txt
#   ├── terminal_log.txt      <-- this run's terminal output, ANSI stripped
#   └── VERSION.txt
#
# Every report therefore carries its own complete terminal output: you can zip
# one folder and hand it over, and nothing from another run is mixed in. The
# folder name is not knowable in advance, so the script fills it in.
LOG_FILE=""
#
# Only change this if you want ONE shared log for the whole machine instead of
# one per report. A fixed path is appended to on every run, and the script
# creates the directory if it does not exist:
#   LOG_FILE="$HOME/Belgeler/systemReport/terminal_log.txt"  # report root
#   LOG_FILE="$HOME/Documents/systemReport/terminal_log.txt" # English Documents
#   LOG_FILE="$HOME/systemReport/logs/terminal.log"          # separate log folder
#   LOG_FILE="$HOME/systemReport.log"                        # plain file in $HOME
#   LOG_FILE="/var/log/systemReport.log"                     # system-wide, needs root
#
# Those are examples only, none of them is active. With the empty value above,
# no path here is used.


# =============================================================================
# end of settings
